# How sensitive are buyers to price?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Why does a fuel price rise barely dent driving this week but change cars bought in five years? What is elasticity, and why does it decide almost everything that follows?"
next: {"title":"Who really pays a tax?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/15-who-really-pays-a-tax.cast"}

## Dearer fuel, same driving
use: line_chart
with:
    x:
      - Jan 2007
      - Feb 2007
      - Mar 2007
      - Apr 2007
      - May 2007
      - Jun 2007
      - Jul 2007
      - Aug 2007
      - Sep 2007
      - Oct 2007
      - Nov 2007
      - Dec 2007
      - Jan 2008
      - Feb 2008
      - Mar 2008
      - Apr 2008
      - May 2008
      - Jun 2008
      - Jul 2008
    series:
      - {name: Pump price, values: [100, 100, 114, 126, 138, 134, 130, 122, 123, 123, 135, 133, 134, 133, 143, 151, 166, 179, 180]}
      - {name: Miles driven, values: [100, 98, 99.9, 98.8, 99.8, 99.5, 99.1, 99.9, 99.5, 99.7, 99.1, 97.2, 99.5, 98.4, 97.8, 97.9, 97.6, 96.9, 96.5]}
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 200
    y_label: Index, Jan 2007 = 100
    x_label: ''
    box:
      x: 90
      'y': 160
      w: 800
      h: 430
sources:
    - id: bls_gas
      title: 'Average Price: Gasoline, Unleaded Regular (Cost per Gallon) in U.S. City Average (APU000074714)'
      authors: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, via FRED
      year: 2024
      finding: $2.27 in January 2007, $4.09 in July 2008 (index 180).
      url: https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/APU000074714
    - id: fhwa_vmt
      title: Vehicle Miles Traveled, seasonally adjusted (TRFVOLUSM227SFWA)
      authors: U.S. Federal Highway Administration, via FRED
      year: 2024
      finding: Monthly miles travelled in July 2008 were 3.5% below January 2007 (seasonally adjusted).
      url: https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/TRFVOLUSM227SFWA
    - id: gdh2004
      title: 'Elasticities of road traffic and fuel consumption with respect to price and income: a review'
      authors: Goodwin, P., Dargay, J. and Hanly, M.
      year: 2004
      finding: A lasting 10% rise in the real fuel price cuts fuel consumption by about 2.5% within a year and about 6% in the longer run (vehicle travel about 1% and 3%).
      doi: 10.1080/0144164042000181725

    dot pt_peak at.data [18,180] cites ["bls_gas"] hidden true
    label lab_peak "180" left attach_to pt_peak font_size 32 hidden true
    dot pt_miles at.data [18,96.5] cites ["fhwa_vmt"] hidden true
    label lab_miles "96.5" below attach_to pt_miles font_size 32 hidden true
    scratch card cites ["gdh2004"] work ["Fuel price +10%","Fuel use, 1 year: −2.5%","Fuel use, long run: −6%"] x 560 y 282 font_size 22 hidden true

    card title "Dearer fuel, same driving"

When petrol gets dearer, how much less do people drive? America ran the test in 2007 and 2008.
    draw axes

This is the average pump price, set to 100 in January 2007. By July 2008 i (@draw pt_peak lab_peak@)t stood at 180: up four-fifths.
    draw line_1

B: Nearly double. People must have left their cars at home.
    highlight line_1

Let's see if you agree. On the same scale, draw how far Americans drove over those months.
    point at.data [9,8] gesture underline duration 3

    ask "Draw the miles-driven line, then press Done." on line_2 from 0 store g
        right: Good eye: you kept it nearly flat, and so did America.
        wrong: Most people draw a clear drop. The real line barely dips.

Here it is. At the price peak, miles driven were down about three and a half percent.
    draw pt_miles lab_miles

B: Three and a half percent, for an eighty percent jump? And a  (@point at.data [11,97.2] gesture circle@)recession had already begun that winter.
    highlight line_2

So even that small dip is not all price.
    fade pt_peak lab_peak to 0.3

Within weeks, driving barely responds: the commute is as long as before, and the car in the drive burns what it burns.
    highlight line_2

B: And five years later?
    point at.data [18,96.5] gesture arrow

Then people can change the car, or where they live.
    fade line_1 pt_peak lab_peak to 0.35

A review of many s (@draw card_box card_line_1@)tudies found that a lasting ten percent rise in fuel prices cuts fuel  (@draw card_line_2@)use about two and a half percent within a year, and a (@draw card_line_3@)bout six percent in the longer run.

B: Same price rise, two very different answers. So 'how sensitive are buyers?' needs a 'given how long?' attached.
    highlight card_line_2 card_line_3

And it needs a number we can compare. That number comes next.
    highlight card_line_1

## One number for sensitivity
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Quantity of fuel (Q)
    y_label: Price (P)
    demand:
      elasticity: 0.25
    supply: {}
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
    supply_shift:
      amount: 0
      label: S′
    change_arrows: true
sources:
    - id: marshall1890
      title: 'Principles of Economics, Book III, chapter IV: The elasticity of wants'
      authors: Marshall, A.
      year: 1890
      finding: Introduces the elasticity of demand as the measure of how much the amount demanded responds to a change in price.
    - id: gdh2004
      title: 'Elasticities of road traffic and fuel consumption with respect to price and income: a review'
      authors: Goodwin, P., Dargay, J. and Hanly, M.
      year: 2004
      finding: A lasting 10% fuel price rise cuts fuel use about 2.5% within a year and about 6% in the longer run.
      doi: 10.1080/0144164042000181725

    portrait marshall cites ["marshall1890"] x 830 y 540 width 200 of "Alfred Marshall" cameo true hidden true
    path ghost_D dashed #b5482e points [[441.2,540.5],[592.6,133.7]] style.opacity 0.3 hidden true
    path spend_old closed dashed #8f887c points [[0,0],[50,0],[50,50],[0,50]] data true hidden true
    path spend_new closed #d0865f points [[0,0],[45.8,0],[45.8,68.6],[0,68.6]] data true style.fill #f2c14e style.opacity 0.45 hidden true
    text spend_lab "spent: P × Q" #8a5a00 at.data [22,8] font_size 28 hidden true
    scratch card cites ["gdh2004"] work ["ε = %ΔQ ÷ %ΔP","fuel, 1 year: −2.5% ÷ 10% ≈ −0.25","|ε| < 1 inelastic,  > 1 elastic"] x 700 y 560 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "One number for sensitivity"

B: Fuel use barely moves in a year, and more than twice as mu (@draw marshall@)ch given time. Alfred Marshall gave that kind of sensitivity a name in 1890: the price elasticity of demand.
    draw axes

B: It's the percent change in quantity divided by  (@erase marshall@)the percent change in price. For fuel within a  (@draw card_line_2@)year: minus two and a half over ten, so about minus a quarter.
    draw card_box card_line_1

Minus a quarter. So a small number means insensitive buyers?
    highlight card_line_2

B: Yes. Ignore the sign: below one, demand is inelastic; above one, elastic. Short-run fuel demand is inel (@draw demand_curve label_D@)astic, and on this diagram that is a steep demand curve.
    draw card_line_3

Now say a war (@draw supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar@) cuts the oil supply. Where does the supply curve go?
    erase card

    ask "Move the supply curve, then press Done." on supply_curve predict true store t
        right: Yes. {t.why}
        wrong: Not quite. {t.why}

B: Left:  (@animate supply_shift.amount -25 duration 2.5@)at every price, less fuel is offered. W (@fade supply_curve label_S to 0.3@)ith steep demand, (@draw shift_equilibrium_point label_E_shift change_arrows@) the price shoots up and the quantity hardly falls.
    draw supply_shift_curve label_S_shift

Because nobody can stop driving to work this week. The price has to climb a long way before enough drivers give up enough fuel.
    highlight price_change_arrow label_dP

B: Now give buyers five years. The  (@hide label_dP@)same su (@animate demand.elasticity 1.5 duration 3@)pply cut, but demand has flattened: the price rises far less, and the quantity falls more.
    draw ghost_D

So elasticity decides how a shock splits: into a higher price, or into less bought.
    highlight change_arrows

B: And it decides the bill. W (@animate demand.elasticity 0.25 duration 2.5@)ith inelastic demand, a dearer go (@show label_dP@)od mea (@draw spend_old@)ns more spent on (@draw spend_new spend_lab@) it: people pay much more and buy almost as much.
    erase ghost_D

    erase spend_old spend_new spend_lab

Try it yourself: pause, and turn the demand curve by dragging near its end. Watch the same supply cut move the price less as demand flattens.
    explore

## Is elasticity a constant?
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Short run, review
      - Short run, corrected
      - Long run, review
      - Long run, corrected
    values:
      - 0.25
      - 0.09
      - 0.6
      - 0.31
    value_labels: true
    colors:
      - grey
      - red
      - grey
      - red
    label_size: 22
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 0.7
    y_label: Elasticity, sign dropped
    box:
      x: 90
      'y': 170
      w: 570
      h: 470
sources:
    - id: gdh2004
      title: 'Elasticities of road traffic and fuel consumption with respect to price and income: a review'
      authors: Goodwin, P., Dargay, J. and Hanly, M.
      year: 2004
      finding: 'Fuel consumption: about −0.25 within a year, about −0.6 in the longer run.'
      doi: 10.1080/0144164042000181725
    - id: hij2012
      title: Demand for gasoline is more price-inelastic than commonly thought
      authors: Havranek, T., Irsova, Z. and Janda, K.
      year: 2012
      finding: 'Meta-analysis of 202 estimates from 41 studies: publication bias roughly doubles published averages; corrected mean elasticities are −0.09 short run and −0.31 long run.'
      doi: 10.1016/j.eneco.2011.09.003
    - id: hks2008
      title: Evidence of a shift in the short-run price elasticity of gasoline demand
      authors: Hughes, J. E., Knittel, C. R. and Sperling, D.
      year: 2008
      finding: US short-run price elasticity −0.21 to −0.34 in 1975–1980, −0.034 to −0.077 in 2001–2006; land use, fewer non-car options and income among the suspected causes.

    scratch card cites ["hks2008"] work ["US, short run","1975–80: 0.21 to 0.34","2001–06: 0.03 to 0.08"] x 805 y 470 font_size 26 hidden true
    text conf1 "More response with time:" x 830 y 520 font_size 28 hidden true
    text conf1b "consensus" #2f7d4f x 830 y 480 font_size 40 hidden true
    text conf2 "How much, here and now:" x 830 y 400 font_size 28 hidden true
    text conf2b "contested" #b5482e x 830 y 360 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "Is elasticity a constant?"

B: One thing nags me about that diagram. It treats fuel's elasticity as a fixed fact of nature.
    draw axes

The reviews do give fairly steady numbe (@draw bar_1@)rs. Drop the minus sign: about a  (@draw bar_3@)quarter in the short run, about point six in the long run.

B: Reviews of published studies. And a clear, sizeable effect is easier to publish than a small, noisy one.
    highlight bar_1 bar_3

That is exactly what Tomas Havranek and two co-authors tested in 2012, on two hundred and two estimates. Guess: once they corrected for it, how big was the long-run number?
    point at.data [3,0.05] gesture tap duration 4

    ask "Drag the corrected long-run bar to your guess." on bar_4 store g
        right: Close: you said {g}, the corrected figure is {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; the corrected figure is {g.true}, a gap of {g.off}.

B: About point three: ha (@draw bar_2@)lf. And the short-run number fell below a tenth.

It moves over time, too. For American drivers, s (@draw card_box card_line_1 card_line_2@)hort-run estimates for the late 1970s ran from about point two to point (@draw card_line_3@) three; for 2001 to 2006, under a tenth.

B: Why would drivers become less responsive? Analysts point to more spread-out suburbs, fewer alternatives to the car, and higher incomes.
    highlight card_line_3

So how sure are we? That buyers respo (@draw conf1 conf1b@)nd more when given time: consensus. How much, in (@draw conf2 conf2b@) a given place and decade: contested.
    erase card

B: Which is why an elasticity should always come with a when and a where.
    highlight bar_2 bar_4

## Math and new cases
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: You could set fuel taxes for a living.
    good: Ask what buyers can switch to, and you are most of the way there.
    poor: Elasticity takes a while to sink in. Like fuel prices.
    none: Some answers only show up in the long run.
domain: {"x":[0,17],"y":[0,13]}

    axes ax x_label "Quantity (Q)" y_label "Price (P)" hidden true
    curve d_steep #b5482e expr "9.5 - 2.4*x" x_from 0.5 x_to 3.6 hidden true
    label lab_steep "this week" right attach_to d_steep hidden true
    curve d_flat #2f6b8f expr "6.2 - 0.45*x" x_from 0.3 x_to 9.5 hidden true
    label lab_flat "years later" above attach_to d_flat hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch math top-right work ["ε = %ΔQ ÷ %ΔP","spending P × Q:  %Δ ≈ %ΔP + %ΔQ","fuel: +10% − 2.5% = +7.5%","ε = −2: +10% − 20% = −10%"] font_size 34 hidden true
    cards sortc bins ["Barely","A lot"] check each hidden true
        * "Table salt" icon salt bin Barely
        * "Petrol this week" icon "fuel pump" bin Barely
        * "Croissants, rival café" icon croissant bin "A lot"
        * "One of three airlines" icon airplane bin "A lot"
    text w_alt "alternatives" #2f6b8f x 780 y 470 font_size 40 hidden true
    text w_time "+ time" #2f6b8f x 780 y 400 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "Math and new cases"

Before the test: the arithmetic behind it, if you want it.
    draw ax d_steep lab_steep d_flat lab_flat

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b

@math

    erase mathq

The elasticity is the percent change in quantity over the percent change in price.
    draw math_box math_line_1

Spending is price times quantity, so its percent change is roughly the two percent changes added.
    draw math_line_2

Fuel: pl (@draw math_line_3@)us ten, minus two and a half, so the bill rises seven and a half percent. With an ela (@draw math_line_4@)sticity of minus two: plus ten, minus twenty, so spending falls ten percent.

    erase math

@test

    erase mathq

    quiz "Harbourtown's ferry raises its fares by 10%, and trips fall by 4%. What happens to the ferry's fare takings?" voice b
        intro: Three new cases to try it on.
        right: They rise, by about 6%: the price rose more than the trips fell.
        wrong: Compare the two percentages: which is bigger, the change in price or the change in trips?
        + They rise
        * They fall
        * They stay the same

    hide ax d_steep lab_steep d_flat lab_flat

    draw sortc

    ask "A 10% price rise: do buyers cut back barely, or a lot? Drop each card in its box." on sortc stage own store f
        right: All {f.total} right: substitutes and time decide it.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try. Ask what else buyers could switch to, and how fast.

    erase sortc

    show ax d_steep lab_steep d_flat lab_flat

    quiz "A city doubles its downtown parking fees. When is the drop in car trips biggest?" voice b
        right: After a few years: people change jobs, homes and habits only slowly.
        wrong: What can people change in a week, and what only in a few years?
        * In the first week
        + After a few years
        * It is the same throughout

So how sensitive are buyers? As sensitive as their al (@draw w_alt@)ternatives and their t (@draw w_time@)ime allow. Whenever a market takes a shock, that is the first thing to ask.
    highlight d_steep d_flat

